c and is shaped by experience—so if girls are steered away from spatial toys, praised less for problem-solving, and subtly discouraged, their brains develop accordingly. It’s a self-fulfilling prophecy. ### Why Do So Many Girls *Feel* They Struggle More? Since there’s no inherent deficit, why does your cousin—and so many other girls—feel out of place and less capable? The answer includes **stereotype threat**, **confidence gap**, and **visibility bias**. - **Stereotype threat:** When a girl is aware of the “girls are bad at math” stereotype, the anxiety of confirming that stereotype actually reduces her working memory and performance on tests. This has been demonstrated in controlled experiments: simply checking a box marked “female” before a math test can lower scores for otherwise high-achieving girls. It’s not that they can’t do the math; it’s that the extra cognitive load of fighting the stereotype holds them back. - **The confidence gap, not the ability gap:** Boys tend to overestimate their math ability, while girls underestimate theirs—even when their actual grades and test scores are identical. This makes girls more likely to feel they “don’t belong” in advanced classes, even when they are fully qualified. Your cousin’s feeling of being out of place is a classic symptom of this, not a signal of incompetence. - **Visibility bias:** Boys in class are often louder in answering questions and more willing to risk being wrong, while girls may stay quiet unless they’re certain. This can make it seem like the boys are understanding faster, when in reality, everyone is struggling. You’re seeing the loudest struggles, not the silent ones. ### So, What About Your Cousin? When you say “isn’t it more likely she’s just not as naturally wired for it,” you’re accidentally endorsing the exact story she already tells herself. If she believes that her gender predetermines her math ability, then every difficult moment becomes proof that she’s inherently lacking, rather than a normal part of learning advanced material. That belief crushes motivation: *Why bother trying if I’m just not built for it?* **My original advice wasn’t dismissing biology—it was pointing out that biology isn’t the barrier. The barrier is this very myth.** Helping her means: - **Explicitly dismantle the myth:** You can say, “You know, I used to think maybe girls just weren’t as good at math. Then I learned that when women and men have the same opportunities, there’s zero difference. The feeling of being an impostor is actually a sign you’re in a challenging place where anyone would struggle—not that you’re the wrong gender for it.” - **Show her real-life counterexamples:** Point her toward the many brilliant women in math and science—modern ones like Maryam Mirzakhani (first woman Fields Medalist), or look up programs like the “Girls’ Angle” math club. Representation matters. Knowing that other women felt the same way and excelled anyway can be incredibly liberating. - **Double down on the “growth mindset” from my first message:** When she struggles, reinforce that it’s a sign of learning, not a sign of genetic failure. The feeling of confusion is the exact feeling of your brain building new connections. I don’t blame you for asking, because we’ve all been steeped in this myth for generations. But if you really want to boost her confidence, the most powerful thing you can do is believe—truly believe—that her math ability is not limited by her chromosomes. She’s not fighting her nature; she’s fighting a cultural headwind. And your belief in her can be a huge part of the tailwind she needs.
