 result? The boy gets more practice, more encouragement, more comfort with the struggle of problem-solving. The girl internalizes that struggle means she's bad at it. By the time your grandmother is assessing who "picks it up faster," she's not observing a level playing field. She's observing the accumulated effects of years of differential treatment, much of it happening right there in her own classroom. ### The self-fulfilling prophecy This is the tragic part: teachers observe boys improving faster, and they conclude boys are naturally better. That belief then further shapes their subtle behaviors (calling on boys more, challenging them more), which *then actually makes the boys improve faster*, confirming the teacher's original belief. The cycle is invisible because it looks like a confirmation of biology, when it's actually a confirmation of bias. Your grandmother's 30 years of seeing this pattern don't prove it's innate; they prove how powerful and consistent the social conditioning is. She saw the same movie play out year after year because the same script was being followed. ### Generational change in teachers themselves What's fascinating is that today, many teachers are explicitly trained to recognize and counter these biases. I've spoken with math teachers who, after making a conscious effort to call on girls equally, to wait a full 10 seconds for answers, to praise girls' reasoning process, and to explicitly teach that struggle is a sign of growth, tell me the perceived gap in their classrooms simply evaporates. If the difference were hardwired, no simple change in teacher behavior could erase it. The fact that it can be erased—and is being erased in many schools—is powerful evidence that the observed difference was never about innate ability. ### Bringing it back to your cousin So when your grandmother and generations of teachers saw boys "getting it" faster, they were likely seeing the fruits of a garden they helped water and weed more carefully than the other side. This doesn't make them bad people or bad teachers. It makes them products of their time, just as we all are. But for your cousin right now, the message is: the very system that produced that observation is the same system that is telling her she doesn't belong. And she can break free of it. Her current teacher, her classmates, maybe even her family, might hold the same unconscious expectations your grandmother did. Knowing that gives her power. She can mentally label that feeling of "I'm just not wired for this" not as a fact about her brain, but as a ghost of a centuries-old story that has nothing to do with her actual potential. Your grandmother wasn't lying. She just didn't have the full picture of cause and effect. We have the benefit of decades of research that she probably wasn't exposed to. And that research says your cousin has every bit as much right to be in that advanced math class as any boy in the room—and the struggles she's feeling are not a sign of her limits, but of a system that never expected her to succeed. You can help her prove it wrong.
