s matters for your cousin Even if we entertained, for the sake of argument, that there’s some tiny biological tilt in average spatial reasoning (and many highly respected psychologists don’t think that’s true), it still wouldn’t explain your cousin’s experience. Here’s why: - The range of ability within women is vastly larger than any average difference between men and women. Your cousin could be in the top 1% of mathematical thinkers regardless of her sex. - Her feeling of being “out of place” and “not very smart” is a classic symptom of impostor phenomenon and internalized stereotype, both of which are purely social-psychological. No amount of prenatal testosterone explains why a high-achieving girl doubts her own intelligence. That is entirely about the messages she has received. - If you tell her, “Well, maybe you’re just not biologically wired for this,” you will cripple her motivation. We know from stereotype threat research that simply reminding someone of their gender before a test can lower their score. Believing in a biological limit becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy. So no, I’m not dismissing biology because I have an agenda. I’m following the science, which has largely abandoned the prenatal testosterone theory as a major cause of math achievement differences. And practically, the most effective way to help your cousin is to treat her struggle as a normal, conquerable challenge — not as a sign of a brain built wrong for math. That’s where real change happens.
