ement accounts for very little. So we would reject the pure social conditioning hypothesis for sprinting. That’s exactly what the science has done. The same process, applied to math, yields the opposite conclusion. ### You can still trust your eyes — with training The problem isn’t that observation is worthless. It’s that unaided observation is very bad at separating correlation from causation, especially when the observer is part of the system generating the correlation. Your eyes tell you the sun moves across the sky; you need scientific reasoning to understand that the Earth is rotating. Your grandmother’s eyes told her boys picked up math faster; we need scientific reasoning to understand that she was watching a self-fulfilling prophecy, not a biological destiny. That’s not an insult to your grandmother. It’s a fact about human beings. We all have cognitive biases shaped by our culture. The scientific method — cross-cultural data, controlled experiments, blind evaluations — is the tool we invented precisely to overcome those biases. It doesn’t ask you to abandon your senses; it asks you to verify what your senses are telling you against a larger, more systematic body of evidence. ### The crucial point for your cousin Even if tomorrow we discovered some tiny, previously hidden biological difference in average spatial processing between men and women, it would still be irrelevant to your cousin as an individual. She’s not a statistical average. She’s a specific person in an advanced class, and her struggle is almost certainly a crisis of confidence, not of innate wiring. The most practical, evidence-based way to help her is to treat that confidence as the real issue, because that’s the variable we know we can change. So no, I’m not saying every difference between groups is just social conditioning. I’m saying *this specific one* — the idea that girls are naturally less capable at math — has been rigorously tested and found to be primarily a social artifact. You can trust your eyes on things where the evidence supports them, like the strength gap. But in this case, your eyes are seeing an optical illusion, and the science gives you the lenses to see through it.
