20:40:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair and honest place to land. You’re appealing to what feels like the most solid, time-tested observation we have: across cultures and centuries, men seem to gravitate toward things and systems, women toward people and words. If something appears everywhere and always, surely it’s just natural. I understand why that feels like common sense. Let me engage with that directly, because I think the real story is more interesting — and ultimately more hopeful for your cousin. ### The past isn’t as uniform as it feels When we say “across all of human history,” we’re usually picturing a specific slice: European agrarian society, urban industrial life, the 1950s household. But anthropology tells a messier story. - **Women as toolmakers and builders:** In many indigenous cultures, women were the primary builders of shelters. Among the Nguni people of Southern Africa, women built the huts. In parts of pre-colonial North America, women constructed and owned the longhouses. - **Women as hunters and warriors:** Archaeological findings increasingly challenge the “man the hunter” narrative. In Peru, a 9,000-year-old burial of a female hunter with a full big-game hunting toolkit was discovered in 2020, and reanalysis of other grave sites suggests that female hunters were not as rare as once believed. In Dahomey, an all-female regiment of elite warriors existed for centuries. - **Men as caregivers and word-people:** The notion that men are only drawn to things isn’t universal either. Men have been primary caregivers, teachers, and nurses in many societies, and the world’s most famous poets and playwrights across history were mostly men — not exactly a sign that men are “naturally” disinclined toward words. What *has* been nearly universal is the division of labor along sex lines, but the content of that division has been remarkably flexible. The constant was not “men build, women care” — it was “whatever men do is valued and visible, and whatever