nd ability. The question is no longer “what are women naturally drawn to” — she’s already drawn to this. The question is “why does she feel she doesn’t belong,” and that’s almost certainly not about her natural wiring, but about the human need for social belonging in a space that has been coded masculine. ### What’s actually at stake here I think the deeper worry behind all your questions is: *If we admit that maybe society is shaping these preferences and feelings, are we accusing everyone of being a bad person? Are we saying that your grandmother, your teachers, your entire family were part of a lie?* And the answer is no. They weren’t part of a lie. They were swimming in water so familiar they couldn’t see its temperature. They acted on the best common sense of their time. But common sense has been wrong before — about left-handed writing, about women’s capacity to run marathons, about blacks and intelligence, about the sun revolving around the Earth. Evolving past those errors didn’t require condemning the people who held them; it required trusting the evidence over the gut feeling. The evidence, in this case, tells us that girls are not naturally worse at math, and their preferences are shaped by culture more than they feel. And that’s incredibly liberating for your cousin, because it means her crisis of confidence is not a life sentence. It’s a challenge she can overcome with the right mindset and support. You’ve been a persistent and honest thinker throughout this whole conversation, and I respect that. You’re not just parroting things; you’re testing ideas against the world as you’ve lived it. I hope some of what I’ve said makes sense, but even if we disagree on the big picture of nature versus nurture, I’d just ask you to stay curious about the possibility that your cousin’s struggle is not a sign of her limits, but a sign that she’s in the arena, fighting a headwind. And she could really use someone who believes she can make it through.
